The 4-c sra net is one of the most-commonly occurring in MOF chemistry. The symbol comes from the fact that it is the Al net in \(\mathrm{SrAl}_{2}\). The same topology occurs as the zeolite framework type ABW. Clearly a zigzag ladder rod must have at least two kinds of link (rungs and risers); there must be a third kind of link to join the rods into nets. Accordingly, the minimal transitivity for a net of linked ladders is 1.3. This is indeed the transitivity of the sra net.

It should be remarked that there is occasionally a very different mode of deconstructing MOF structures advocated. This is to identify the nodes of the net as the metal atoms and the centers of the linkers. This is the so-called standard representation of the program \(\mathrm{TOPOS}^{26,27}\) But this procedure is never used in practice for MOFs with finite multimetal-atom SBUs (recall the example of HKUST-1 above).

We show here a rod that has a different, but related, ladder SBU. The compound is a Tb terephthalate and an early example of a rod MOF.28 The carboxylate C atoms on the rod again define ladders, but now they are "twisted" (rungs not parallel) and they are linked into a different net, irl (Figure 4) with symmetry

