4. MOFs WITH SBUS OF EDGE-SHARED TETRAHEDRA In contrast to ladders in which rungs fall on (almost) parallel lines, patterns in which rungs are on lines at \(90^{\circ}\) to their neighbors are really rods of tetrahedra sharing opposite edges. In such a rod,

which we discuss next, there must be at least two kinds of edge and each vertex is connected to five others on the net, formed with ditopic linkers will be 6-c and have minimal transitivity 1 3. We indeed find such a net.

Tetrahedra can also form rods by sharing faces. The structure is then related to the BC helix described in section 1.5. We discuss MOFs with such SBUs in section 13.1

