# 8. MOFS WITH SBUS OF FACE-SHARED TETRAGONAL PRISMS

Rod SBUs in this category are composed of either linked tetragonal prisms or antiprisms and are all connected by facesharing. They can be found in carboxylates as well as sulfonates and are usually 5-c in the case of regular prisms or 7-c in the case of antiprisms.

# 8.1. MOFs with SBUs of Face-Shared Tetragonal Prisms and Ditopic Linkers

Face-sharing tetragonal prism SBUs of formula \(\left[\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}(-\mathrm{COO})_{4}\right]_{\infty}\) linked by simple ditopic BDC produced a framework, termed MIL-140A, \(\mathrm{ZrO}(\mathrm{BDC})\) .215 Other isoreticular variants were obtained by replacing BDC through longer linkers (MIL-140B-D). Figure 55 shows the rod-SBU, where each \(Z\mathbf{r}^{4+}\) is seven coordinated with three \(\mu_{3}\) -oxygen and four carboxylate oxygen. The higher hydrolytic stability of this MOF, in comparison to UiO-66,216 was attributed to the inorganic rod \(Z\mathbf{r}\) oxide chains

versus the isolated \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\) clusters. The SBU was described as either a corner-sharing double chain or chains of edge-sharing dimers of zirconium-polyhedra, which the authors simplified to a uninodal 6-c \(\beta\) -Sn topology, bsn.217 In accordance with our approach, the SBU is deconstructed differently, leading to a 5-c gui net with transitivity 3 6 and symmetry Imma. The facesharing tetragonal prisms run along [010] in undulating fashion and are linked through their shortest inter-rod distances.

In contrast, face-sharing tetragonal antiprism SBUs of the same formula, \(\left[\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{O}(-\mathrm{COO})_{4}\right]_{\infty}\), were formed when angular \(m\mathrm{BDC}\) was used instead of linear BDC (Figure 56).218 The so-produced framework with formula \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}(m\mathrm{BDC})_{2}\) contains \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) that are coordinated in a distorted octahedral environment with four oxygen from four different carboxylate groups and two \(\mu_{3}\)-oxygen. The carboxylate C atoms form tetragonal antiprism SBUs. The authors have also discussed the structure as double-chains, similar to what was detailed above for the gui net. After deconstruction of this framework in the same manner, we
